'''''Berberis oiwakensis''''' is a species of [[plant]] in the barberry family, [[Berberidaceae]]. It is native to [[Taiwan]], [[China]] ([[Guizhou]], [[Hong Kong]], [[Sichuan]], [[Xizang]] ([[Tibet]]) and [[Yunnan]]) and [[Myanmar]], where it occurs at elevations of 600 to 3800 m.<ref name="efloras">[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=2&taxon_id=200008403 Flora of China v 19 p 774],</ref> It has recently been found [[Naturalisation (biology)|naturalized]] in [[South Africa]].<ref>Jaca, T. P. 2017. Chinese hollygrape (''Mahonia oiwakensis''): newly detected weed. South African Plant Invaders Atlas (SAPIA) News 45:2-3. Last accessed February 15, 2018, from http://www.arc.agric.za/ARC%20Newsletters/SAPIA%20NEWS%20No.%2045,%20July%202017.pdf.</ref>

== Description ==
''Berberis oiwakensis'' is a shrub or tree up to 7 m tall. Leaves are up to 45&nbsp;cm long, compound with 12-20 pairs of leaflets plus a larger terminal one, dark green above, yellow-green below. The [[inflorescence]] is a [[Fascicle (botany)|fascicled]] [[raceme]] up to 25&nbsp;cm long. The berries are egg-shaped, dark blue, sometimes almost black, up to 8&nbsp;mm long.<ref name="efloras" /><ref name="Hayata" />

== Taxonomy ==
''Berberis oiwakensis'' was first scientifically described and named ''Mahonia oiwakensis'' by [[Bunzō Hayata]] in 1916.<ref name="POWO">{{cite POWO |id=1000126-1 |title=''Berberis oiwakensis'' (Hayata) Laferr. |access-date=16 November 2023}}</ref><ref name="Hayata">{{Cite journal|last=Hayata|first=Bunzo|date=1916|title=Contributions to the Flora of Formosa|url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/66589#page/15/mode/1up|journal=Icones Plantarum Formosanarum Nec non et Contributiones Ad Floram Formosanam|volume=6|pages=1–163|via=Biodiversity Heritage Library}}</ref> One year later [[Hisayoshi Takeda]] described a species that he named ''Mahonia lomariifolia''.<ref name="POWO" /><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Takeda|first=Hisayoshi|date=1917|title=Contributions to the Knowledge of the Old World Species of the Genus Mahonia|url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/100888#page/235/mode/1up|journal=Notes from the Royal Botanic Garden, Edinburgh|volume=6 |issue=29–30|pages=209–248|via=Biodiversity Heritage Library}}</ref> Under that name, it was considered to occur only in mainland China, while ''Mahonia oiwakensis'' was limited to Taiwan. Modern taxonomic sources unite the two as a single species with [[Principle of Priority|priority]] going to the first description.<ref name="POWO" /> Because of differences between the Taiwanese and mainland Chinese plants, Julian Shaw described them as separate [[subspecies]] of ''Mahonia oiwakensis'' in 2011.<ref name="POWO" /><ref name="Shaw">{{Cite journal|last=Shaw|first=Julian|date=2011|title=Developments in Mahonia|url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/about-the-rhs/publications/magazines/the-plantsman/2011-issues/march/Developments-in-Mahonia.pdf|journal=The Plantsman |series=New Series|volume=10|pages=44–49}}</ref> However, this has not yet been accepted by the majority of botanists. In addition, there has been a longstanding disagreement on if ''[[Mahonia]]'' should be more properly classified as a [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] of ''[[Berberis]]''. A paper was published by [[Joseph Edward Laferrière]] in 1997 summarized the arguments in favor of classification in ''Berberis'' and published a new name for the species, ''Berberis oiwakensis''.<ref name="POWO" /><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Laferrière |first1=Joseph Edward |title=Transfer of Specific and Infraspecific Taxa from ''Mahonia'' to ''Berberis'' (Berberidaceae) |journal=Botanicheskii Zhurnal |date=1997 |volume=82 |issue=9 |pages=95–98 |url=http://en.arch.botjournal.ru/?t=issues&id=19970909&rid=pdf_0005055 |access-date=15 November 2023 |archive-date=7 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231107012402/http://en.arch.botjournal.ru/?t=issues&id=19970909&rid=pdf_0005055 |url-status=dead }}</ref> As of 2023, this is the most widely accepted classification.<ref name="POWO" /><ref>{{cite web |last1=WFO |title=''Berberis oiwakensis'' (Hayata) Laferr. |url=https://www.worldfloraonline.org/taxon/wfo-0000563615 |website=World Flora Online |access-date=16 November 2023 |date=2023}}</ref>

=== Hybrids ===
''Berberis oiwakensis'' is one parent of the important garden [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] [[Berberis × hortensis|''Berberis'' × ''hortensis'']], which includes popular [[cultivar]]s such as 'Charity', 'Winter Sun' and 'Lionel Fortescue' (the other parent is ''[[Berberis japonica]]'').<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Brickell|first=C.D.|date=1979|title=The hybrids between Mahonia japonica and M. lomariifolia|journal=The Plantsman|volume=1|pages=12–20}}</ref> It is also a parent of the cultivar 'Arthur Menzies', though with ''[[Berberis bealei]]'' as the other parent.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Witt|first=J.A.|date=1967|title=Mahonia 'Arthur Menzies'|journal=Arboretum Bulletin Washington|volume=Spring|pages=14–15}}</ref> In the wild in Taiwan, ''Berberis oiwakensis'' appears to hybridize with wild ''Berberis japonica''.<ref name="Shaw" />

''Berberis oiwakensis'' subsp. ''lomariifolia'' has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/314114/i-Mahonia-oiwakensis-i-subsp-i-lomariifolia-i/Details
| title = RHS Plantfinder - ''Mahonia oiwakensis'' subsp. ''lomariifolia'' | access-date=1 April 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web
| url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 
